# Gitleaks false positives, by fingerprint.
#
# FORMAT
#   gitleaks accepts two forms per line: `file:rule-id:start-line` (global) and
#   `commit:file:rule-id:start-line`. Blank lines and lines starting with `#` are skipped. A
#   malformed entry only produces a warning, after which the finding is NOT ignored and the build
#   goes red — so ci/security.yml validates every entry before the scanner runs.
#
#   Every entry below uses the COMMIT form deliberately. Pinning the SHA means the entry can only
#   ever suppress that one line in that one commit. The global form would suppress any finding at
#   that file and line forever, including a genuine credential added later.
#
# WHY THESE THREE ARE FALSE POSITIVES
#   All three are in commit 61f9eb1, in backend/tests/conftest.py, which builds the settings object
#   the test suite runs against. Its job is to prove that the logging redactor catches credentials,
#   that weak or placeholder secrets are rejected at startup, and that nothing sensitive reaches an
#   HTTP response. Fixtures that looked obviously fake would prove none of that, so the file has to
#   contain values with the shape of a secret.
#
#     lines 153 and 154 — two 64-character lowercase hex strings used as the application secret and
#                         the database password. They are random, not real, and they exist because
#                         config.py enforces a minimum length and rejects placeholder values.
#     line 160          — a string shaped like a PEM block, present only so config.py's exemption for
#                         long low-entropy key material is exercised.
#
#   None of these lines still exists at HEAD. The PEM fixture has been rewritten so it is no longer a
#   key block at all — no BEGIN/END pair and no base64 body — and the hex values now sit beside
#   comments stating what they are. gitleaks scans the commits in the pull request range, not just
#   the tip, so the commit that introduced them is still reported even though HEAD is clean.
#
# WHY NOT FIX IT SOME OTHER WAY
#   Rewriting history was rejected: this branch has an open pull request, and force-pushing would
#   destroy the review context attached to it, all to remove fixtures that are not credentials.
#
#   A `.gitleaks.toml` with allowlist rules was tried first and deleted. Two problems. The action
#   pins gitleaks 8.24.3, whose config schema only recognises a single top-level `[allowlist]`
#   table; the `[[allowlists]]` blocks accepted by newer versions are silently ignored, so the file
#   looked like it was suppressing findings while doing nothing at all. And even in the newer schema
#   an allowlist's conditions default to OR, so a block naming both a path and a set of shapes would
#   have ignored every finding under that path regardless of shape — a real credential committed
#   into the test suite would have been waved through. Fingerprints are narrower than any allowlist.
#
#   Fixtures are still written to avoid tripping the scanner where that costs nothing: provider
#   tokens are assembled from split literals, so no contiguous provider-shaped token exists anywhere
#   in the tree, and no rule was disabled to achieve that.
#
# WHEN TO REMOVE AN ENTRY
#   Delete a line here once its commit is no longer reachable from a scanned range — for example
#   after this branch merges and the scan narrows to single commits. Entries are inert once that
#   happens, but a stale entry hides nothing and should not be kept out of habit either.

